<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Coverage settings for the XPlat Code Coverage collector.

  Purpose: keep the denominator to code we actually author. Before this file, ~2100 of the ~13300
  measured lines were Roslyn source-generator output — chiefly a 1958-line RegexGenerator DFA in the
  Website plugin — which is emitted, not written, and cannot be meaningfully unit-tested. Leaving it
  in cost roughly ten percentage points and hid the real picture (51.5% raw vs 61.2% for real code).

  It also narrows the measurement to code the test suite can actually guard on the CI box. Two
  categories are excluded by file, by the author's decision:

    1. Views/** — view code-behind and compiled XAML. The pointer-driven parts (the drag ghost, the
       manual resize grips, window chrome) need real pixel hit-testing against a live window server,
       which the headless runtime cannot reproduce. Note the views are STILL tested: UI/ViewLoadTests
       instantiates every window and page and asserts it loads and binds, which is what catches the
       runtime XAML failures that matter. Only the metric's scope changed, not the coverage.

    2. Platform OS integration that needs a specific OS or a live desktop session — the Windows and
       macOS notifiers, the Windows shortcut COM path, run-at-startup, the tray and the taskbar
       progress. These cannot execute on the Linux CI runner at all, and run-at-startup would mutate
       the developer's real "launch at login" setting just by running the suite.

  Deliberately NOT excluded, so this stays a scope decision rather than a way to flatter the number:
  SingleInstanceService (loopback IPC, genuinely testable and tested), Program.cs/App.axaml.cs, and
  every network-bound service (UpdateFlow/UpdateService/PluginCatalogService/DialogHelper/CliRunner) —
  those are ordinary app code that simply needs seams to reach, not code the suite is barred from.

  Use it with:  dotnet test … (see CONTRIBUTING) with: settings coverlet.runsettings
-->
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="XPlat code coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura</Format>
          <!-- Generated code carries [GeneratedCode]; hand-written code marked
               [ExcludeFromCodeCoverage] is opted out deliberately. -->
          <ExcludeByAttribute>GeneratedCodeAttribute,CompilerGeneratedAttribute,ExcludeFromCodeCoverageAttribute</ExcludeByAttribute>
          <!-- Belt and braces: the regex generator's output lives under obj/ and is *.g.cs. Source
               generators do not always stamp every emitted member with [GeneratedCode]. -->
          <ExcludeByFile>**/obj/**/*.g.cs,**/*.g.cs,**/*.Designer.cs,**/Views/**,**/Services/WindowsNotifier.cs,**/Services/MacNotifier.cs,**/Services/StartMenuShortcut.cs,**/Services/StartupService.cs,**/Services/TrayService.cs,**/Services/TaskbarProgressService.cs</ExcludeByFile>
          <!-- The test project itself is not the subject under measurement. -->
          <Exclude>[Downloader.Desktop.Tests]*,[xunit.*]*</Exclude>
          <IncludeTestAssembly>false</IncludeTestAssembly>
          <SkipAutoProps>true</SkipAutoProps>
          <DeterministicReport>false</DeterministicReport>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
